package main

import (
	"github.com/candacelabs/csf/pkg/gotth/live"
	"github.com/candacelabs/csf/pkg/widget"
)

// templ itself is not imported here: the generator adds "github.com/a-h/templ"
// to every file it writes, so naming it again is a redeclaration in the
// generated output rather than a missing import.

// Page is the whole document: the live library's shell, this host's own head
// content, and one section per registered widget.
//
// It knows nothing about what a widget is. It is handed already-rendered
// regions, in registration order, and puts them on the page — which is the
// property that makes adding a second widget a registration rather than a page
// edit.
//
// app is the first parameter because Document is a method, and it is a method
// because what it puts in the head depends on Config.Dev: this host sets Dev,
// so the same call that renders the runtime tag also renders the session
// inspector's and the dev-reload tag, in the order the inspector needs.
templ Page(app *live.App[widget.HostState, live.AnonymousIdentity], regions []templ.Component) {
	@app.Document(mountPath, "Widget SDK demo", templ.Attributes{"lang": "en"}, headExtras()) {
		<main>
			<h1>Widget SDK</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				One binary, one registry, one goroutine per session. Both cards
				below were generated from widget documents &mdash; the scenes,
				the motion, the legend, the stats and every word in them &mdash;
				and this page knows nothing about either.
			</p>
			for _, region := range regions {
				@region
			}
			<p class="status">connection: <span class="dot"></span></p>
			<p class="hint">
				The raft card is drawing an election that is actually
				happening: three goroutines in this process hold terms and cast
				votes at each other over channels, and one heartbeat round is
				one snapshot is one round of pulses. When the leader is killed
				the pulses stop because the survivors really are campaigning.
				The node card&rsquo;s data is a timer, because that widget has
				nothing behind it yet. Neither widget opened either stream
				&mdash; a widget document names no host, no address and no
				credential. The button pauses the pulses, and a viewer who has
				asked for reduced motion never sees them at all.
			</p>
		</main>
	}
}

// headExtras is this host's own head content, which live.Document knows nothing
// about and is not entitled to: a viewport meta and a stylesheet are decisions
// about how a page looks, not about how it is connected.
templ headExtras() {
	<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
	<link rel="stylesheet" href="/widget.css"/>
}
